  "content": {
    "id": "p29-q12-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "12",
    "prompt": "Triangle $ABC$ has vertices $A(-1,-3)$, $B(4,2)$ and $C(12,-6)$. By calculating gradients show that $\\triangle ABC$ is right-angled.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$m_{AB}=1$, $m_{BC}=-1$, $m_{AC}=-\\frac{3}{13}$.\n\nSince $m_{AB}m_{BC}=-1$, $AB\\perp BC$ and $\\angle ABC=90^\\circ$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}m_{AB}&=\\frac{2-(-3)}{4-(-1)}\\\\m_{AB}&=1\\\\m_{BC}&=\\frac{-6-2}{12-4}\\\\m_{BC}&=-1\\\\m_{AC}&=\\frac{-6-(-3)}{12-(-1)}\\\\m_{AC}&=-\\frac{3}{13}\\end{align*}$$\n$$m_{AB}m_{BC}=1\\times(-1)=-1.$$\nTherefore $AB\\perp BC$, so $\\angle ABC=90^\\circ$ and $\\triangle ABC$ is right-angled at $B$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 58
  },
